## SilkTerm - Settings dialog declarations.
##
## This file IS the dialog: which rows exist, in what order, under which
## heading, on which tab, what each one edits, and when each is grayed out.
## It is compiled into the binary, so an edit needs a rebuild - and the tests
## fail if a row names a setting the code does not know, or leaves one out.
##
## Measurements are DIP: a CSS pixel, 1/96 inch. The layout is solved in that
## space and multiplied by the display's scale factor on the way to the
## screen, so these numbers mean the same physical size at any DPI.
##
## What is NOT here: how a value is read, written or formatted. Those go
## through typed fields with transforms (a stored millisecond becomes a 1..100
## slider, a fraction becomes a percent), which is code, not data.

## •••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••
## Tabs
## •••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••

## Left to right along the top. Every heading below names one of these.
tabs: "Silk", "Background", "Text", "Cursor", "Movement", "Themes", "Window", "Shell", "Keys"

## •••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••
## Layout
## •••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••

## Nearly every measurement here is a FLOOR, not a fixed size: content that
## needs more room gets it (a wide label pushes the panel wider, a tall UI font
## makes rows taller). So a value set too small loses to the content and one
## set too large just gives a roomier dialog - neither can break the layout.

layout:

	## Panel
	width: 540                 ## floor; the widest label, button row or tab strip wins
	pad: 18                    ## margin inside all four panel edges
	tabs_gap: 10               ## tab strip -> first row
	buttons_gap: 28            ## last row -> footer buttons

	## Rows
	row_height: 32             ## floor for an ordinary row
	row_pad: 12                ## row grows to the UI line height plus this
	header_height: 36          ## floor for a section heading (its rule sits at the bottom)
	header_pad: 14             ## heading grows to the UI line height plus this
	header_gap: 24             ## extra space above a heading that follows a section
	subgroup_gap: 8            ## extra space above a row that leads a sub-group
	indent: 16                 ## label offset per sub-group level (controls do not move)
	label_width: 168           ## floor for the label column
	label_gap: 14              ## clear space after the longest label
	revert_width: 22           ## right-edge revert-to-default icon column

	## Controls
	slider_width: 220
	swatch: 20                 ## color chip; also the size of every square control (checkbox, radio)
	hex_width: 92              ## "#rrggbb" field beside a color chip
	value_width: 56            ## numeric field beside a slider
	radio_box: 16              ## radio indicator square
	radio_pitch: 96            ## per radio option: box + label + gap
	dual_pitch: 118            ## per checkbox + label pair on a two-part row
	dropdown_width: 208        ## collapsed dropdown box (fits its longest option)
	dropdown_pair_width: 150   ## floor for each half of a two-dropdown row
	dropdown_item_pad: 8       ## popup row grows to the UI line height plus this
	dropdown_item_min: 24
	base_line_height: 19       ## the UI line height the four pitches above were drawn for

	## Editable fields
	field_height: 26           ## floor; a field grows to the UI line height plus field_pad_v twice
	field_pad: 8               ## text inset from the left and right outlines
	field_pad_v: 4             ## clear space above and below the text line
	caret_pad: 6               ## kept clear right of the caret at end-of-text
	view_ahead: 28             ## characters kept visible ahead of a travelling caret
	edit_menu_width: 132       ## the field's right-click menu

	## Shells grid (the Shell tab: one line per stored shell). The columns are
	## floors and the command takes whatever width is left over, so a wider panel
	## goes to the one value that is routinely too long to read at a glance.
	shell_name_width: 128
	shell_command_width: 190
	shell_seen_width: 78       ## the read-only "Last seen" date
	shell_active_width: 44     ## wide enough for the column title, not the checkbox
	shell_col_gap: 8
	shell_grip: 16             ## the drag handle that reorders a line
	shell_button: 18           ## the remove icon box
	shell_head_gap: 5          ## column titles -> first entry
	shell_add_gap: 10          ## last entry -> the Add button

	## Footer buttons
	button_height: 30          ## floor
	button_pad: 24             ## clear space around the longest caption
	button_width: 76           ## floor
	button_gap: 10

	## Tabs. They sit on the gutter strip at the top of the panel, standing on
	## the line that separates it from the rows below - so they are shorter than
	## a footer button and start a little below the panel's own edge.
	tab_pad: 22                ## clear space either side of a tab's title
	tab_height: 24             ## floor
	tab_pad_v: 6               ## tab grows to the UI line height plus this
	tab_top: 5                 ## panel edge -> top of the tabs
	tab_gap: 4                 ## between tab buttons

	## Scrollbar (shown only when a tab is taller than the panel)
	scrollbar_width: 8
	scrollbar_inset: 5         ## gap between the bar and the panel edge. The bar
	                           ## lives in the panel's own padding, so this has to
	                           ## leave room between it and content that fills that
	                           ## padding - the Shell tab's last column does.
	scrollbar_thumb_min: 24

	## Color picker (the box a color chip opens). Every one of these is a floor:
	## the square gives way first on a narrow panel, since it is the one piece
	## with no text in it.
	pick_gap: 12               ## between the square, the strip and the value column
	pick_strip: 22             ## width of the hue strip
	pick_label_width: 96       ## the "Saturation %" column
	pick_field_width: 58       ## the value boxes
	pick_min_square: 80
	pick_marker: 13            ## the ring on the square

## Flyover help for the footer buttons. Every other tip is the `help:` line on
## the row it belongs to.
help:
	cancel: "Discard every change and close Settings."
	apply: "Apply changes now, without closing Settings."
	ok: "Apply changes and close Settings."

## Chrome glyphs.
icons:
	dropdown_arrow: "▾"   ## in the collapsed box
	dropdown_check: "✓"   ## marks the current value in the open list
	revert: "↺"           ## revert-to-default, one per row

## •••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••
## Rows
## •••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••

## In screen order. A row's NAME is the setting it edits, spelled as the code
## spells it; `kind: heading` starts a GROUP and says which tab it goes on,
## and every row after it belongs to that group.
##
## A SUB-GROUP is not declared. It is a row followed by rows at a greater
## `indent:` - the leader's own label stays put and its members' labels step
## right, so the run reads as belonging to it. Only labels move; every control
## keeps its column. A row back at the shallower depth ends the sub-group.
##
## kind:      heading | toggle | slider | color | text | radio | dropdown |
##            pair | buttons | shells | hotkey
## label:     what the row is called on screen
## setting:   the config.shcl path the revert arrow comments out
## range:     slider low, high
## whole:     slider steps in whole numbers
## scale:     log for a slider whose range spans orders of magnitude, so each
##            doubling gets the same travel. Linear when left out.
## typed_max: how far a value typed in the number box may go past the
##            slider's end. Only for a setting with no % and no real limit of
##            its own. The handle sits at the end and the box shows the number.
##            The config has to read the same value back, which a test checks.
## indent:    sub-group depth of the label (default 0)
## beside:    draw this row on the line above rather than under it. A line's
##            rows split the control column evenly; the first keeps the label
##            column, and its label has to name the whole line unless each
##            row after it names itself. Only a toggle may do that, with its
##            label before its box. A line of nothing but toggles packs at its
##            own width; in a group with other rows its first box keeps the
##            control column.
## options:   the choices, in order, for radio / dropdown / pair
## parts:     the two settings a `pair` row carries, one per option
## key:       only when the row's name cannot be the setting's - because a
##            heading already took that name, or because the row edits no
##            single setting of its own (`key: none`)
## help:      flyover text, for a control whose label does not explain it
## revert_help: flyover for the revert arrow, where it does more than put the
##            shipped default back
## windows:   true for a row that exists only on Windows. On a heading it takes
##            the whole group, and every row in it has to say so as well.
## not_macos: true for a row the macOS build can never use, left out there.
## warning:   draws a warning mark after the label, for a row that may not
##            work on every desktop. The text goes on the end of `help:`, and
##            that one tip shows over the label, the mark and the control
## windows_warning: the same in the Windows build, in place of `warning:`. A
##            mark that applies only there has only this one.

rows:

	## ---------------------------------------------------------------------- Silk

	## One setting that governs the two sections below plus rows on the
	## Background and Cursor tabs. While it is anything but Custom those rows
	## show the profile's values, and their flyover says so; changing one takes
	## the profile to Custom with the values that were on screen.
	Performance:
		kind: heading
		label: Performance
		tab: Silk

	PerfAuto:
		label: Choose automatically
		kind: toggle
		setting: performance.automatic
		help: "Pick the profile for this machine, and step it down when the display cannot keep up with a scroll. A step goes no lower than Low and lasts until SilkTerm restarts."

	PerfProfile:
		label: Profile
		kind: dropdown
		options: "Custom", "Max silk", "High", "Low", "Standard terminal", "Remote (temporary)"
		indent: 1
		setting: performance.profile
		help: "What the look is allowed to cost, from Max silk with every effect at its shipped setting down to Standard terminal with no smooth scrolling at all; Custom uses your own values. Picking one here switches off Choose automatically, and so does changing any setting a profile governs. Remote (temporary) is the exception: it is Standard terminal for a remote screen and lasts only for this session, so it leaves that switch alone."

	## The label stays short because the widest one on any tab sets the panel's
	## width, and the flyover carries what it leaves out.
	PerfCheckHardware:
		label: Check for hardware change
		kind: toggle
		setting: performance.check_hardware
		help: "Notice a different processor, graphics adapter or amount of memory, and rate the machine again. Rating is a few seconds of measuring behind a banner. Only used while the profile above is chosen automatically."

	PerfCheckNext:
		label: Re-test next run
		kind: toggle
		beside: true
		setting: performance.check_next_run
		help: "Rate the machine again at the next launch, whether or not it changed. Clears itself once that launch has done it."

	TextReadability:
		kind: heading
		label: Text readability
		tab: Silk

	TextScrim:
		label: Text scrim
		kind: toggle
		setting: text.scrim.enabled
		help: "A soft halo behind the glyphs, so text stays readable over a busy wallpaper."

	ScrimStrength:
		## percent, where each 20% is one doubling of the finished halo's opacity
		label: Strength %
		kind: slider
		range: 0, 100
		whole: true
		indent: 1
		setting: text.scrim.strength
		help: "How solid the halo gets. High values close the gaps between glyphs into a plate. Light mode quiets it, since a pale patch shows more against a darkened background than a dark one does."

	ScrimRadius:
		label: Scrim radius px
		kind: slider
		range: 0, 20
		whole: true
		indent: 1
		setting: text.scrim.radius
		help: "How far the halo reaches out from the ink."

	ScrimSoftness:
		label: Softness %
		kind: slider
		range: 0, 100
		whole: true
		indent: 1
		setting: text.scrim.softness
		help: "How gradually the halo fades out toward its edge."

	## The two together describe one curve, so they share a line. The label names
	## both; each box says which of them it is by what it holds.
	ScrimFunction:
		label: Function, falloff
		kind: dropdown
		options: "Distance field", "Distance transform", "Dilate + feather", "Gaussian [ugly]"
		indent: 1
		setting: text.scrim.function
		help: "How the halo's shape is computed from the glyphs."

	ScrimRamp:
		label: ""
		kind: dropdown
		options: "Exponential", "Half-normal", "Logarithmic", "Sigmoid", "Linear"
		beside: true
		setting: text.scrim.ramp
		help: "The curve the halo fades along."

	MinContrast:
		## percent of the lightness range, not a WCAG ratio - see palette::readable
		label: Minimum contrast %
		kind: slider
		range: 0, 60
		whole: true
		indent: 1
		setting: text.min_contrast
		help: "Lighten text too dark to read on a dark background, and darken it on a light one. Light themes hold text a little further off. Text set to exactly the background color stays hidden. 0 leaves every color alone."

	## Its own one-row group under the scrim: it works with the halo or without it.
	Outline:
		label: Outline px
		kind: slider
		range: 0, 4
		whole: true
		setting: text.outline
		help: "A crisp outline drawn hard against the glyphs. Works on its own or on top of the halo."

	Scrolling:
		kind: heading
		label: Scrolling
		tab: Silk

	SmoothScroll:
		label: Smooth scrolling
		kind: toggle
		setting: scroll.smooth
		help: "Ease the view instead of jumping a whole line at a time. Off makes every setting below inert."

	## The five below are ordered as one output burst unfolds: it leaves rest,
	## accelerates, tops out, winds down, stops. All five read the same way -
	## higher is faster.

	ScrollEaseIn:
		label: Ease-in %
		kind: slider
		range: 1, 100
		whole: true
		indent: 1
		setting: scroll.ease_in_ms
		help: "How sharply the view leaves a standstill when output starts."

	ScrollRampUp:
		label: Ramp-up %
		kind: slider
		range: 1, 100
		whole: true
		indent: 1
		setting: scroll.ramp_up_ms
		help: "How quickly the view picks up speed once it is moving."

	SingleScreenTau:
		label: Single-screen speed %
		kind: slider
		range: 1, 100
		whole: true
		indent: 1
		setting: scroll.single_screen_tau_ms
		help: "Top speed while the output still fits on one screen."

	ScrollRampDown:
		label: Ramp-down %
		kind: slider
		range: 1, 100
		whole: true
		indent: 1
		setting: scroll.ramp_down_ms
		help: "How hard the view brakes as the output runs out."

	ScrollEaseOut:
		label: Ease-out %
		kind: slider
		range: 1, 100
		whole: true
		indent: 1
		setting: scroll.ease_out_ms
		help: "How crisply the view settles on the final line."

	## ---------------------------------------------------------------- Background

	## A heading whose label is its own tab's title says nothing the tab strip
	## has not, so it draws nothing - but it still assigns the rows below it.
	Background:
		kind: heading
		label: Background
		tab: Background

	BgEnabled:
		label: Wallpaper
		kind: toggle
		setting: wallpaper.enabled

	BgImage:
		label: File or folder
		kind: text
		indent: 1
		setting: wallpaper.image, wallpaper.rotate.folder
		help: "An image, or with Rotate folder on, a folder of them. A named image wins over the folder."

	BgFit:
		label: Fit
		kind: radio
		options: "Stretch", "Zoom"
		indent: 1
		setting: wallpaper.default_fit
		help: "What to do when the image and the window are different shapes. An image with its own Fit tag overrides this."

	BgHonorXmp:
		label: Honor layout tags
		kind: toggle
		indent: 1
		setting: wallpaper.honor_xmp
		help: "Let an image's own embedded Fit and Anchor tags decide how it is placed."

	BgRotate:
		label: Rotate folder
		kind: toggle
		indent: 1
		setting: wallpaper.rotate.enabled
		help: "Treat the path above as a folder and show a different image from it each time."

	BgOpacity:
		label: Visibility %
		kind: slider
		range: 0, 100
		whole: true
		indent: 1
		setting: wallpaper.opacity
		help: "How strongly the wallpaper shows through the terminal background. Light mode mixes in more of it to reach the same visible result, so the number means the same thing in either mode. While Honor look tags is on, an image's own Opacity tag wins over this, so it only applies to images without one."

	BgBlur:
		label: Blur px
		kind: slider
		range: 0, 50
		typed_max: 100
		whole: true
		indent: 1
		setting: wallpaper.blur
		help: "While Honor look tags is on, an image's own Blur tag wins over this, so it only applies to images without one."

	BgHonorXmpLook:
		label: Honor look tags
		kind: toggle
		indent: 1
		setting: wallpaper.honor_xmp_look
		help: "Let an image's own Opacity and Blur tags win over the two sliders above, so the sliders only apply to images without them. The bundled wallpapers are all tagged."

	BgContrastMask:
		label: Contrast mask
		kind: toggle
		setting: wallpaper.contrast_mask.enabled
		help: "Quieten the wallpaper only where it would fight the text, leaving the rest alone."

	BgContrastSize:
		label: Size %
		kind: slider
		range: 0, 100
		whole: true
		indent: 1
		setting: wallpaper.contrast_mask.size
		help: "How large an area each point of the wallpaper is judged against."

	BgContrastStrength:
		label: Strength %
		kind: slider
		range: 0, 100
		whole: true
		indent: 1
		setting: wallpaper.contrast_mask.strength
		help: "How far the mask is allowed to push the wallpaper down."

	BgContrastAuto:
		label: Automask mix %
		kind: slider
		range: 0, 100
		whole: true
		indent: 1
		setting: wallpaper.contrast_mask.auto
		help: "How much of the masking is left to the picture itself rather than the setting above."

	## Last on the tab, since it is the one that depends on the desktop.
	Transparency:
		label: Transparency
		kind: toggle
		setting: transparency.enabled
		help: "See through the terminal background. On Windows this takes effect on the next launch."
		warning: "Transparency relies on the desktop compositor, and may not work on every desktop."
		windows_warning: "Transparency relies on the desktop compositor, and may not work on every desktop. It also keeps a window in view from giving back its graphics memory when idle."

	Opacity:
		label: Opacity %
		kind: slider
		range: 0, 100
		whole: true
		indent: 1
		setting: transparency.opacity

	BackdropBlur:
		label: Blur-behind
		kind: toggle
		indent: 1
		setting: transparency.blur_behind
		help: "Blur whatever is behind the window, where the desktop can do it."

	## ---------------------------------------------------------------------- Text

	Font:
		kind: heading
		label: Font
		tab: Text

	SystemFont:
		label: Use system font
		kind: pair
		options: "Face", "Size"
		parts: SystemFont, SystemFontSize
		setting: font.use_system_family, font.use_system_size
		help: "Follow the desktop's own monospace font, for the face and the size independently."

	FontFamily:
		label: Family
		kind: text
		setting: font.family

	FontSize:
		label: Size
		kind: slider
		range: 6, 40
		typed_max: 128
		whole: true
		setting: font.size

	LineHeight:
		label: Line height
		kind: slider
		range: 0.8, 2
		typed_max: 4
		setting: font.line_height_scale
		help: "Space between lines, as a multiple of the font's own."

	## -------------------------------------------------------------------- Cursor

	Cursor:
		kind: heading
		label: Cursor
		tab: Cursor

	CursorBlink:
		label: Blink rate ms
		kind: slider
		range: 100, 3000
		typed_max: 10000
		whole: true
		setting: cursor.blink_rate_ms
		help: "One full animation cycle."

	CursorHeight:
		label: Height %
		kind: slider
		range: 1, 100
		whole: true
		setting: cursor.size.height
		help: "Cursor height as a percentage of the cell, measured up from the bottom."

	CursorWidth:
		label: Width %
		kind: slider
		range: 1, 100
		whole: true
		setting: cursor.size.width
		help: "Cursor width as a percentage of the cell, measured from the left."

	CursorAnimation:
		label: Animation
		kind: dropdown
		options: "None", "Phase", "Pulse vertical", "Pulse horizontal", "Pulse both"
		setting: cursor.animation

	CursorResume:
		label: Inactivity timer s
		kind: slider
		range: 0.1, 60
		typed_max: 3600
		indent: 1
		setting: cursor.animation_resume_s
		help: "How long typing has to stop before the animation picks up again. Output does not wait this out."

	CursorVisibility:
		## Two independent switches sharing one label; the row itself edits
		## nothing, so its own name is not a setting.
		key: none
		label: Visibility
		kind: pair
		options: "Scrim", "Outline"
		parts: CursorScrim, CursorOutline
		setting: cursor.scrim, cursor.outline

	CopyOnSelect:
		label: Copy on select
		kind: toggle
		setting: shell.copy_on_select
		help: "Put selected text on the clipboard as soon as it is selected."

	## ------------------------------------------------------------------ Movement

	Movement:
		kind: heading
		label: Movement
		tab: Movement

	WheelLines:
		label: Wheel lines
		kind: slider
		range: 1, 10
		typed_max: 100
		whole: true
		setting: scroll.wheel_lines
		help: "Lines traveled per notch of the mouse wheel."

	Scrollbar:
		label: Scrollbar
		kind: toggle
		setting: scroll.scrollbar.enabled

	ScrollbarThickness:
		label: Width px
		kind: slider
		range: 4, 64
		whole: true
		indent: 1
		setting: scroll.scrollbar.thickness

	ScrollbarAutoHide:
		label: Hide when idle
		kind: toggle
		indent: 1
		setting: scroll.scrollbar.auto_hide
		help: "Fade the scrollbar out while the view sits still at the bottom."

	Minimap:
		label: Minimap
		kind: toggle
		setting: scroll.minimap.enabled
		help: "A miniature of the whole scroll buffer beside the text. It takes the room it uses, so the grid loses columns."

	MinimapWidth:
		label: Width px
		kind: slider
		range: 24, 400
		whole: true
		indent: 1
		setting: scroll.minimap.width

	## -------------------------------------------------------------------- Themes

	Themes:
		kind: heading
		label: Themes
		tab: Themes

	Theme:
		label: Theme
		kind: dropdown
		setting: theme
		help: "The color scheme the terminal and this dialog follow."

	ThemeMode:
		label: Mode
		kind: dropdown
		options: "Dark", "Light", "System"
		indent: 1
		setting: theme_mode
		help: "Which variant of the theme to use. System follows the desktop."

	ThemeActions:
		label: ""
		kind: buttons
		options: "Save", "Save as…", "Rename…", "Delete"
		help: "Save keeps the colors below under this theme's name. Only a saved theme can be renamed or deleted."

	Colors:
		kind: heading
		label: Colors
		tab: Themes

	ColBg:
		label: Terminal background
		kind: color
		setting: colors.background

	ColFromWallpaper:
		label: Text colors from wallpaper
		kind: toggle
		indent: 1
		setting: colors.from_wallpaper
		help: "Pick the text and cursor colors from the picture behind them: as far from its brightest areas as they can get, in a hue complementary to its own. The two rows below gray out, and nothing derived this way is saved."

	ColFg:
		label: Foreground
		kind: color
		indent: 1
		setting: colors.foreground

	ColCursor:
		label: Cursor
		kind: color
		indent: 1
		setting: colors.cursor

	ColDialogBg:
		label: Dialog background
		kind: color
		setting: colors.dialog_background

	ColDialogFg:
		label: Dialog text
		kind: color
		indent: 1
		setting: colors.dialog_foreground

	ColMenuBg:
		label: Menu background
		kind: color
		indent: 1
		setting: colors.menu_background

	ColMenuFg:
		label: Menu text
		kind: color
		indent: 1
		setting: colors.menu_foreground

	ColGutter:
		label: Gutter
		kind: color
		indent: 1
		setting: colors.gutter
		help: "Chrome that holds no control, such as the strip these tabs sit on."

	ColHighlight:
		label: Highlight
		kind: color
		indent: 1
		setting: colors.highlight
		help: "Marks several things at once - the live pane's ring, slider handles, revert arrows, the default button."

	ColFocus:
		label: Focus
		kind: color
		indent: 1
		setting: colors.focus
		help: "Marks only the one control the keyboard is on."

	ColScrollbarThumb:
		label: Scrollbar handle
		kind: color
		setting: colors.scrollbar_thumb
		help: "The scrollbar and the minimap marker. These two stay neutral whatever the theme, so a saved theme leaves them out."

	ColScrollbarTrough:
		label: Track
		kind: color
		indent: 1
		setting: colors.scrollbar_trough

	## -------------------------------------------------------------------- Window

	Window:
		kind: heading
		label: Window
		tab: Window

	RememberSize:
		label: Remember last size
		kind: toggle
		setting: window.remember_size
		help: "Reopen at whatever size the window was last closed at, ignoring Columns and Rows."

	RememberPerMonitor:
		label: Per monitor
		kind: toggle
		indent: 1
		setting: window.remember_per_monitor
		help: "Keep a size for each monitor, told apart by its resolution, scale and physical size. A window moved to another monitor takes that monitor's size once it stops there."

	Columns:
		label: Columns
		kind: slider
		range: 20, 400
		typed_max: 1000
		whole: true
		indent: 1
		setting: window.columns

	Rows:
		label: Rows
		kind: slider
		range: 6, 120
		typed_max: 1000
		whole: true
		indent: 1
		setting: window.rows

	RememberMaximized:
		label: Remember maximized
		kind: toggle
		setting: window.remember_maximized
		help: "Reopen maximized when the window was last closed maximized."

	TabRegularWidth:
		label: Tab width regular %
		kind: slider
		range: 4, 100
		whole: true
		setting: window.tab_regular_width_pct
		help: "How wide a tab is when nothing is pushing on it, as a percentage of the window. A crowded bar shrinks tabs below this, and the ones that no longer fit become a page."

	TabMaxWidth:
		label: Tab width max %
		kind: slider
		range: 4, 100
		whole: true
		setting: window.tab_max_width_pct
		help: "How far a tab may grow past its regular width when its text needs the room."

	NewTabNextToCurrent:
		label: New tabs go next to current
		kind: toggle
		setting: window.new_tab_next_to_current
		help: "Open a new tab just right of the one in front. Off puts it at the end."

	Margin:
		label: Margin px
		kind: slider
		range: 0, 40
		typed_max: 200
		whole: true
		setting: window.margin
		help: "Clear space between the text and the window edge."

	TabText:
		kind: heading
		label: Tab text
		tab: Window

	## The four share one line and one revert arrow. Each names itself, and each
	## keeps its own flyover.
	TabShowsTitle:
		label: Program-defined
		kind: toggle
		setting: window.tab_shows_title
		help: "Let a title the running program asked for name the tab. Only a name typed on the tab outranks it, and a narrow tab falls back to the other three."

	TabShowsShell:
		label: Shell
		kind: toggle
		beside: true
		setting: window.tab_shows_shell
		help: "Name the shell the tab is running. The tab's flyover names every source whatever these say, so turning one off hides it without losing it."

	TabShowsProgram:
		label: Program
		kind: toggle
		beside: true
		setting: window.tab_shows_program
		help: "Name whatever the shell is running, or last ran."

	TabShowsDirectory:
		label: Directory
		kind: toggle
		beside: true
		setting: window.tab_shows_directory
		help: "Name the directory the shell is sitting in. It shortens from the right as the tab gets narrower."

	WindowTitle:
		kind: heading
		label: Window title
		tab: Window

	TitleShowsTab:
		label: Show the tab's text
		kind: toggle
		setting: window.title_shows_tab
		help: "Let the title bar fall back to what the active tab says, including a name typed on it. A title the running program asked for still comes through either way."

	Hyperlinks:
		kind: heading
		label: Hyperlinks
		tab: Window

	HyperlinksEnabled:
		key: Hyperlinks
		label: Enabled
		kind: toggle
		setting: hyperlinks.enabled

	LinkOpenCommand:
		label: Open command
		kind: text
		indent: 1
		setting: hyperlinks.open_command
		help: "What to run for a clicked link. Empty hands it to the desktop."

	## What a window costs the machine. Last on the tab, since it is the part
	## that depends on the graphics driver.
	ResourceUse:
		kind: heading
		label: Resource use
		tab: Window

	IdleRelease:
		label: Free resources when idle
		kind: toggle
		setting: window.idle_release
		help: "Give the graphics card's memory back after the window has sat unused for a while, and take it back the moment it is used again, without stopping anything running in it. This helps most with many windows open on a card with little memory, or next to programs that use a lot of it."
		warning: "Turn it off only if the graphics driver has trouble with it."

	IdleHiddenMin:
		label: Minutes when hidden
		kind: slider
		range: 1, 1440
		scale: log
		typed_max: 10080  ## a week
		whole: true
		indent: 1
		setting: window.idle_release_hidden_min
		help: "How long a minimized or covered window waits. On Windows, taking the graphics card back on a restore can take about a second."

	IdleMin:
		label: Minutes otherwise
		kind: slider
		range: 1, 1440
		scale: log
		typed_max: 10080  ## a week
		whole: true
		indent: 1
		setting: window.idle_release_min
		help: "How long a window that is only unfocused, with nothing printing, waits. Longer, since it may be on a second screen being read. On Windows with Transparency on, a window still on screen is never let go, since it would turn black."

	SoftwareRendering:
		label: Always use software rendering
		kind: toggle
		setting: window.software_rendering
		not_macos: true
		help: "Draw on the processor rather than the graphics card. Slower, but uses none of the card's memory. A window whose card cannot give it what it needs falls back to this on its own."

	## --------------------------------------------------------------------- Shell

	Shell:
		kind: heading
		label: Shell
		tab: Shell

	## The shells the Tabs menu offers under "New tab with shell", in the order it
	## offers them - and the first switched-on one is the shell a plain new tab,
	## pane or window runs. The list fills itself in a few seconds after launch;
	## this grid is where it is ordered, renamed, switched off and pruned.
	Shells:
		label: ""
		kind: shells
		help: "The topmost switched-on shell is the default for new tabs, panes and windows."

	## The lowest-precedence answer to "where does this shell start". A new tab,
	## pane or window inherits the directory of the pane it came from, and a
	## SilkTerm launched from a shell keeps that shell's directory - so this is
	## what a launch from the desktop, a menu or a shortcut gets. A --directory
	## on the command line beats all of them.
	StartupDir:
		kind: heading
		label: "Default startup directory"
		tab: Shell

	StartupDirectory:
		label: Directory
		kind: text
		setting: shell.startup_directory
		help: "Where a shell starts when nothing else has said. Understands ~ and environment variables. Overridden by --directory on the command line, by an inherited directory, and by the directory SilkTerm was launched from."

	## The two things SilkTerm sets up in a shell it starts. PowerShell is the
	## one shell that cannot be asked where it is - it keeps its location to
	## itself - so it is offered a block for its profile that says so, and a
	## profile that already reports is left alone. The bash prompt is an offer
	## through the environment; an rc file with a prompt of its own wins.
	IntegrationHead:
		kind: heading
		label: "Shell integration"
		tab: Shell

	ShellIntegration:
		label: "Update PowerShell profiles"
		kind: toggle
		setting: shell.integration
		help: "Add a directory-reporting block to each PowerShell profile, so a new tab, pane or window opens where that shell is. Other shells need nothing. A profile that already reports is left alone, and deleting the block switches it off for good."

	BashPrompt:
		label: "Use git-aware Bash prompt"
		kind: toggle
		setting: shell.bash_prompt
		help: "Give each new bash pane the x9ps1-git prompt, which shows the branch and status of the git project you are in. It replaces a prompt set in .bashrc, and nothing is written into any file."

	## What Explorer opens a file type with, per user. Register writes it and
	## saves what was there; the revert arrow puts that back. Both act at once,
	## so Cancel does not undo them. Neither is a setting in the config file:
	## the registry is the state, and the arrow is lit while it says SilkTerm.
	OpenWith:
		kind: heading
		label: "Open with SilkTerm"
		tab: Shell
		windows: true

	OpenBatch:
		label: "Batch files"
		kind: buttons
		options: "Register"
		windows: true
		help: "Run .bat and .cmd files in SilkTerm when they are opened from Explorer. The pane stays open when the script ends, showing how it exited. Register again after moving SilkTerm."
		revert_help: "Put back whatever opened .bat and .cmd files before SilkTerm was registered."

	OpenPowerShell:
		label: "PowerShell scripts"
		kind: buttons
		options: "Register"
		windows: true
		help: "Run .ps1 files in SilkTerm when they are opened from Explorer, with PowerShell 7 if it is installed and Windows PowerShell if not. A script runs unless the machine allows only signed ones, the same as Windows' own Run with PowerShell."
		revert_help: "Put back whatever opened .ps1 files before SilkTerm was registered."

	OpenVbScript:
		label: "VBScript files"
		kind: buttons
		options: "Register"
		windows: true
		help: "Run .vbs files in SilkTerm when they are opened from Explorer, through the console script host, so their messages print in the pane instead of popping up."
		revert_help: "Put back whatever opened .vbs files before SilkTerm was registered."

	OpenFolder:
		label: "Folder menu"
		kind: buttons
		options: "Register"
		windows: true
		help: "Add Open in SilkTerm to the right-click menu of folders and drives, and of the empty space in a folder. On Windows 11 it is under Show more options."
		revert_help: "Take Open in SilkTerm off the folder menu."


	## ---------------------------------------------------------------------- Keys

	## Every hotkey under `keys:` in the config file, grouped the way the menu
	## bar groups what they do. A row is set by pressing the new chord in its box;
	## the file keeps the same spellings a hand edit would. The tab is a long list
	## and scrolls, so it is left out of the dialog's height, as the Shell tab is.
	KeysFile:
		kind: heading
		label: File
		tab: Keys

	HotkeyNewWindow:
		label: "New window"
		kind: hotkey
		setting: keys.new_window

	HotkeySettings:
		label: "Settings"
		kind: hotkey
		setting: keys.settings

	HotkeyQuit:
		label: "Quit"
		kind: hotkey
		setting: keys.quit

	KeysEdit:
		kind: heading
		label: Edit
		tab: Keys

	HotkeyCopy:
		label: "Copy"
		kind: hotkey
		setting: keys.copy

	HotkeyPaste:
		label: "Paste"
		kind: hotkey
		setting: keys.paste

	KeysView:
		kind: heading
		label: View
		tab: Keys

	HotkeyFontBigger:
		label: "Increase font size"
		kind: hotkey
		setting: keys.font_bigger

	HotkeyFontSmaller:
		label: "Decrease font size"
		kind: hotkey
		setting: keys.font_smaller

	HotkeyFontReset:
		label: "Reset font size"
		kind: hotkey
		setting: keys.font_reset

	HotkeyFullscreen:
		label: "Fullscreen"
		kind: hotkey
		setting: keys.fullscreen

	HotkeyContextMenu:
		label: "Right-click menu"
		kind: hotkey
		setting: keys.context_menu
		help: "Opens the right-click menu on the focused pane, the way the Menu key does by default."

	KeysTabs:
		kind: heading
		label: Tabs
		tab: Keys

	HotkeyNewTab:
		label: "New tab"
		kind: hotkey
		setting: keys.new_tab

	HotkeyCloseTab:
		label: "Close tab"
		kind: hotkey
		setting: keys.close_tab

	HotkeyPrevTab:
		label: "Previous tab"
		kind: hotkey
		setting: keys.previous_tab

	HotkeyNextTab:
		label: "Next tab"
		kind: hotkey
		setting: keys.next_tab

	HotkeyMoveTabBack:
		label: "Move tab left"
		kind: hotkey
		setting: keys.move_tab_back

	HotkeyMoveTabForward:
		label: "Move tab right"
		kind: hotkey
		setting: keys.move_tab_forward

	KeysPanes:
		kind: heading
		label: Panes
		tab: Keys

	HotkeySplitRight:
		label: "Split vertical"
		kind: hotkey
		setting: keys.split_right

	HotkeySplitDown:
		label: "Split horizontal"
		kind: hotkey
		setting: keys.split_down

	HotkeyClosePane:
		label: "Close pane"
		kind: hotkey
		setting: keys.close_pane

	HotkeyFocusLeft:
		label: "Go to pane on left"
		kind: hotkey
		setting: keys.focus_left

	HotkeyFocusRight:
		label: "Go to pane on right"
		kind: hotkey
		setting: keys.focus_right

	HotkeyFocusUp:
		label: "Go to pane above"
		kind: hotkey
		setting: keys.focus_up

	HotkeyFocusDown:
		label: "Go to pane below"
		kind: hotkey
		setting: keys.focus_down

## •••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••
## Graying
## •••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••

## A control is grayed out while any setting listed against it is off - a
## toggle that is false, or a number sitting at zero. A leading "!" reverses
## it: grayed while that setting is ON. Listing several means all must hold.
##
## Not listed here: the two system-font switches, which the environment grays
## when the desktop reports no font for them to follow. That is a fact about
## the machine, so it is answered in code.

gates:

	PerfCheckHardware: PerfAuto
	PerfCheckNext: PerfAuto

	BgImage: BgEnabled
	BgRotate: BgEnabled
	BgOpacity: BgEnabled
	BgBlur: BgEnabled
	BgFit: BgEnabled
	BgHonorXmp: BgEnabled
	BgContrastMask: BgEnabled
	BgContrastSize: BgEnabled, BgContrastMask
	BgContrastStrength: BgEnabled, BgContrastMask
	BgContrastAuto: BgEnabled, BgContrastMask
	Opacity: Transparency
	BackdropBlur: Transparency

	ScrimStrength: TextScrim
	ScrimRadius: TextScrim
	ScrimSoftness: TextScrim
	ScrimFunction: TextScrim
	ScrimRamp: TextScrim
	## MinContrast sits under the scrim but is not gated by it: the lift is
	## applied to the text itself, halo or no halo.
	CursorScrim: TextScrim
	CursorOutline: Outline    ## an outline to join, before it can join one

	ColFg: !ColFromWallpaper
	ColCursor: !ColFromWallpaper

	FontFamily: !SystemFont

	IdleHiddenMin: IdleRelease
	IdleMin: IdleRelease
	FontSize: !SystemFontSize

	RememberPerMonitor: RememberSize
	Columns: !RememberSize
	Rows: !RememberSize

	ScrollEaseIn: SmoothScroll
	ScrollRampUp: SmoothScroll
	SingleScreenTau: SmoothScroll
	ScrollRampDown: SmoothScroll
	ScrollEaseOut: SmoothScroll

	ScrollbarThickness: Scrollbar
	ScrollbarAutoHide: Scrollbar

	MinimapWidth: Minimap

	## The two scrollbar colors are deliberately ungated: the minimap's marker and
	## its own bar take them too, so they still do something with the scrollbar off.

	LinkOpenCommand: Hyperlinks
